# Team Quiz App — Self-Paced, Timed (Streamlit)

A self-hosted, PowerApps/forms-style quiz. You (the **Admin**) prepare the
questions once and set a turnaround time (default 60 minutes). You share a
single link + join code with the whole team. Each person starts whenever
suits them — their personal clock starts at that moment, runs for the full
duration even if they close the tab, and their quiz auto-submits the instant
their time is up. Every answer autosaves as they pick/type it, so nothing
is lost if they get pulled away.

Questions can be **multiple choice** (auto-graded) or **free text**
(auto-graded against expected answers if you provide any, otherwise flagged
for you to review manually).

## 1. Install

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

## 2. Set your admin password (do this before sharing the link!)

The admin area is hidden from participants — it only appears at a separate
URL (`?admin=1`) and is password-gated. Set the password one of two ways:

**Option A — quick/local testing:** edit `app.py` and change this line near
the top:
```python
ADMIN_PASSWORD = "admin123"  # <-- CHANGE ME
```

**Option B — recommended for anything shared:** create
`.streamlit/secrets.toml` next to `app.py`:
```toml
ADMIN_PASSWORD = "something-only-you-know"
```
This keeps the password out of the code entirely (and is how you'd set it
on Streamlit Community Cloud too, via the app's Secrets settings).

## 3. Run locally

```bash
streamlit run app.py
```

- **Participant link** (share this with the team): the base app URL, e.g.
  `http://localhost:8501` — or with the join code prefilled:
  `http://localhost:8501/?code=12345`. This view has no admin option
  anywhere on it.
- **Admin link** (keep this to yourself): `http://localhost:8501/?admin=1`
  — asks for the password before showing any controls.

## 4. Admin workflow

Once logged in at the `?admin=1` link, there are three tabs:

- **➕ Create quiz** — set title + turnaround time (minutes), add MCQ or
  free-text questions one at a time, then Publish to get a join code.
- **✏️ Edit quiz** — pick any existing quiz, change its title, duration, or
  score-visibility setting, remove a question (🗑️ next to it) or add new
  ones, then **Save changes**. If people have already started that quiz,
  you'll see a warning: adding questions or fixing typos is safe, but
  removing/reordering questions can shift what earlier answers line up
  with — fine for a quiz that hasn't started yet, worth avoiding mid-flight.
- **📊 Dashboard** — pick a quiz to see live status per participant (in
  progress with time left / submitted / expired), auto-graded scores, a
  "Full answer detail" expander with every response (including free-text
  ones to grade manually) and CSV downloads for both the summary and the
  detail view. At the bottom, a **Danger zone** lets you clear all results
  for that quiz (attempts + answers) while keeping the quiz itself intact —
  handy for a dry run before the real thing. It requires an explicit
  confirmation checkbox and cannot be undone.

## 5. Navigation & UI

Participants move through questions one at a time with **Previous / Next**
buttons — no free jumping around. The last question shows **✅ Final
Submit** instead of Next; if anything's still unanswered at that point,
it's flagged (but Final Submit still goes through, so no one gets stuck).

Streamlit's default chrome — the hamburger menu, footer, and top-right
toolbar (which includes the "view source" GitHub icon on apps deployed
from a public repo) — is hidden via CSS on every page, so participants
never see a way to peek at the underlying code.

## 6. Question types

- **Multiple choice** — 2 to 4 options, one marked correct. Auto-graded.
- **Free text** — participant types their answer. Optionally give one or
  more expected answers (comma-separated, case-insensitive exact match)
  for auto-grading, e.g. `Paris, paris, city of light`. Leave it blank and
  the question is simply collected for manual review — it won't count
  toward the auto-graded score, but every response is visible in the
  dashboard's detail view and CSV export.

## 7. Let colleagues on the same network join

If everyone is on the same office Wi-Fi:

```bash
streamlit run app.py --server.address 0.0.0.0
```

Then find your machine's local IP (e.g. `192.168.1.42`) and share
`http://192.168.1.42:8501` as the participant link.

## 8. Let remote colleagues join (no local network needed)

The easiest free option is **Streamlit Community Cloud**:

1. Push this folder to a GitHub repo.
2. Go to https://share.streamlit.io, sign in with GitHub, and deploy the
   repo. Add `ADMIN_PASSWORD` under the app's Secrets settings there
   (same TOML format as step 2, Option B).
3. Share the public participant URL (e.g.
   `https://your-quiz.streamlit.app`) with the team, and keep
   `https://your-quiz.streamlit.app/?admin=1` for yourself.

Alternatively, a quick tunnel like `ngrok` or `cloudflared` works for a
one-off session without deploying anywhere.

## 9. Capturing answers & the 1-hour TAT

Every answer is written to `quiz.db` (SQLite) the instant a participant
picks or types it — not just on final submit — so nothing is lost if their
browser crashes or they get pulled into a meeting.

The turnaround time is enforced **per participant**, not globally:

- Each person's `start_time` is stamped only once, the first time they
  click "Start quiz."
- Their deadline = `start_time + duration_minutes`.
- If they close the tab and reopen the link hours later, the app recomputes
  remaining time from their original `start_time` — it does not give them
  a fresh 60 minutes.
- The moment their remaining time hits zero (checked whenever they load the
  page, or via the periodic auto-refresh while it's open), their attempt is
  automatically marked "submitted" with whatever they'd answered so far.

## 10. Notes & limits

- `quiz.db` is created next to `app.py` on first run — delete it to reset
  absolutely everything (all quizzes and all results). To reset just one
  quiz's results and keep its questions, use the Dashboard's Danger zone
  instead.
- Auto-submit on timeout triggers when the participant's page is open (it
  checks on every load and every auto-refresh tick). If someone starts and
  never reopens the tab again before or after their deadline, their
  attempt shows "time expired" on the dashboard but formally flips to
  "submitted" the next time anyone touches that row. For strict
  enforcement with zero user interaction, add a small scheduled job (e.g.
  a cron task) that calls `mark_submitted` for any attempt whose deadline
  has passed — the function is already in `app.py`.
- This uses SQLite, which is fine for a single running app instance (one
  `streamlit run` process, or one Streamlit Cloud app). If you need
  multiple server instances behind a load balancer, swap the DB calls in
  the "DATA LAYER" section of `app.py` for Postgres/MySQL.
- The admin password gate is basic (a single shared password, checked
  client-side against a string in your app/secrets) — fine for keeping
  casual participants out of the admin view, not a substitute for real
  auth if the quiz involves sensitive data.
